  "content": {
    "id": "p28-q18-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "18",
    "prompt": "Show that\n$$\\frac{1}{\\frac{1}{\\log_a(x)}+\\frac{1}{\\log_b(x)}}=\\log_{ab}(x).$$",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "Change base so the denominator is $\\log_x(a)+\\log_x(b)=\\log_x(ab)$. Its reciprocal is $\\log_{ab}(x)$.",
      "worked": "The expressions are defined for $a,b,x>0$ with $a,b,x,ab\\ne1$. Change base and apply the product law.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{1}{\\frac{1}{\\log_a(x)}+\\frac{1}{\\log_b(x)}}&=\\frac{1}{\\log_x(a)+\\log_x(b)}\\\\\\frac{1}{\\frac{1}{\\log_a(x)}+\\frac{1}{\\log_b(x)}}&=\\frac{1}{\\log_x(ab)}\\\\\\frac{1}{\\frac{1}{\\log_a(x)}+\\frac{1}{\\log_b(x)}}&=\\log_{ab}(x).\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 33
  },
